# Example ReRDMFT input file: LiH, 6-31G, GNOF, FULL_OPTIMIZATION TRUE with ORBITAL_OPTIMIZER
# ADAM_NEO -- the hybrid counterpart of lih_gnof_c4_full_optimization.inp (ADAM) and
# lih_gnof_c4_neo_full_optimization.inp (NEO), same system, same functional, including C4_SPINOR.
# Every macro-iteration re-decides its driver from the PREVIOUS iteration's own |dE|: ADAM while
# |dE| > ADAM_NEO_SWITCH_TOLERANCE (default 5e-3 Ha, after 3 fixed initial ADAM macro-iterations), NEO once it drops to or below that, and back
# to ADAM whenever it grows above it again -- iteration 1 always starts on ADAM, since there is no
# previous |dE| yet. Watch the "driver" column of the iteration table switch back and forth (this
# system switches several times per method), and the "switching orbital optimizer: ..." log lines
# each time it does: DEBUG TRUE (set here) makes those lines also print the Kramers-pair deviation
# (X2C, C4_SPINOR) or spin deviation (NON_REL) of the integrals at the exact moment they are handed
# from one optimizer's parametrization to the other's -- ADAM and NEO reach the same
# Kramers-/spin-restricted subspace through different parametrizations (see
# Utils/KramersRestriction.h), and this is the first place in the project that hands the SAME
# integrals back and forth between them within a single run, so it is checked directly on the data
# at every handoff rather than only trusted from the construction. All three methods converge to
# the same energies as the ADAM-only and NEO-only files (to ~1e-9 Hartree), and the final
# "Final test: all checks PASSED" confirms Kramers/spin pairing survived every switch.
NELEC 4
BASIS lih-6-31g.gbs
NON_RELATIVISTIC TRUE
X2C TRUE
C4_SPINOR TRUE
FUNCTIONAL GNOF
PNOF_SUBSPACES 2
PNOF_COUPLING 2
FULL_OPTIMIZATION TRUE
ORBITAL_OPTIMIZER ADAM_NEO
USE_RI FALSE
DEBUG TRUE

GEOMETRY
Li  0.000000   0.000000   0.000000
H   0.000000   0.000000   1.594900
END
